        "original_id": "d8c3029b-5988-54f9-86c1-b66fb03d9dd8",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: John O'Hara, Rabindranath Tagore, Hason Raja, Elizabeth Appleton\n- Roles: writer, polymath, Nobel laureate\n- Works: Elizabeth Appleton\n- Places: America, Oxford University\n- Concepts: social status, manners, social realism, Contextual Modernism\n\nFacts:\n- Rabindranath Tagore was born on 7 May 1861 and died on 7 August 1941.\n- Rabindranath Tagore was a Bengali polymath known as Gurudev.\n- Rabindranath Tagore reshaped Bengali literature and music and influenced Indian art through Contextual Modernism in the late 19th and early 20th centuries.\n- John Henry O'Hara was born on 31 January 1905 and died on 11 April 1970.\n- John O'Hara was an American writer best remembered for observing social status and manners in early to mid-20th-century America and for depicting social realism.\n- John O'Hara published the novel Elizabeth Appleton in 1963.\n- Hason Raja gained international recognition a few years after his death when Rabindranath Tagore mentioned him in lectures at Oxford University.",
